From: OpenWrt Contributor <contributor@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH 0/3] ar934x NAND: fix UBI rootfs creation on ath79 boards

Hi,

This series fixes a long-standing NAND boot failure on ath79 boards that
use the AR934x/QCA95xx NAND controller with software ECC (all MikroTik
NAND RouterBOARDs, e.g. RB951G-2HnD, and the Meraki MR18).

Symptom
-------

After installing OpenWrt on NAND the board boot-loops:

  ubi0 error: ubi_read_volume_table: the layout volume was not found
  ubi0 error: ubi_attach_mtd_dev: failed to attach mtd2, error -22
  VFS: Cannot open root device "" ... Kernel panic
  Rebooting in 1 seconds..

The kernel itself is loaded fine by RouterBOOT; only the rootfs cannot be
mounted. Flashing from the initramfs appears to succeed (ubiformat and the
volume creation complete), but the very next boot cannot read the UBI.

Root cause
----------

The AR934x NAND controller only performs ECC on full-page accesses and
cannot write subpages (this is documented in ar934x_nfc_setup_hwecc()).
However, for software ECC the driver still advertises subpage support, so
UBI creates a *subpage* layout (VID header offset 512). That layout cannot
be written/read reliably by this controller, so after the first reboot the
EC/VID headers are unreadable and the layout volume is "not found".

The same fix was applied once in 6561ca1fa51 ("ath79: ar934x: fix mounting
issues if subpage is not supported") and then reverted in cb9ccd644bf9
("ath79: ar934x: still advertise subpage on soft ecc") because the Meraki
MR18 ships a *pre-existing* vendor UBI that uses the subpage layout.

The fix
-------

Disable subpage writes for 2048-byte pages by default (the controller
cannot do them anyway), and let boards that must keep a pre-existing
subpage-layout UBI opt out via "qca,nand-subpage-writes". The Meraki MR18
sets that property, so it keeps its current behaviour; MikroTik boards get
a UBI layout that can actually be written.

Patch 3 is needed because yafut (used for MikroTik NAND since 23.05)
relies on subpage writes for the final partial chunk of the kernel file
and would otherwise fail with -EINVAL. kernel2minor + nandwrite write raw
OOB data and do not depend on subpage writes; this is also what the old
ar71xx target and RouterBOOT v6 expect (an ELF-in-YAFFS file named
"kernel").

Patches
-------

  0001 mtd: rawnand: ar934x: disable subpage writes for 2048-byte pages
  0002 ath79: meraki-mr18: keep the vendor subpage write layout
  0003 ath79: mikrotik: write NAND kernel images with kernel2minor

Testing
-------

Tested on a MikroTik RouterBOARD 951G-2HnD (Samsung 2048+64 NAND,
RouterBOOT 6.48.6, u-boot/target ath79/mikrotik):

  - TFTP-boot the initramfs, `sysupgrade -n` the squashfs image, reboot.
  - UBI now attaches with "VID header offset: 2048 (aligned 2048)".
  - "VFS: Mounted root (squashfs filesystem) readonly".
  - /overlay is a UBIFS volume on /dev/ubi0_1 and the board survives
    repeated reboots.
  - Verified with kernel 6.6 (24.10) and 6.12 (25.12.5).

Built and booted from NAND on both:
  target/linux/ath79 (subtarget mikrotik), profile
  mikrotik_routerboard-951g-2hnd.

Notes
-----

  - "qca,nand-subpage-writes" is a driver-private boolean, not a mainline
    binding; a binding update for qca,ar934x-nand should accompany this if
    the driver ever moves upstream.
  - Patch 3 restores the pre-yafut NAND write path. If the maintainers
    prefer to keep yafut, the alternative is to teach yafut to pad its
    final chunk to a full page so it works with subpage writes disabled.
  - Nothing changes for hardware-ECC boards: NAND_NO_SUBPAGE_WRITE was
    already set for ECC engine type ON_HOST.

Thanks,
